15. Outcome Attribution

This is a major protection against bad learning.

CNS must not assume:

WIN → GOOD DECISION
LOSS → BAD DECISION

Instead:

OUTCOME
 ↓
ATTRIBUTION ANALYSIS
 ↓
candidate causes
 ↓
confounders / luck / regime
 ↓
counter-evidence
 ↓
attribution confidence
 ↓
lesson candidate
 ↓
verification
 ↓
memory update

Possible causes:

Factor	Examples

Decision quality	Was decision reasonable with information available then?
Execution	Slippage, rejection, latency, fill quality
Regime	Trend, range, volatility, liquidity
External event	Unexpected news / structural event
Luck / variance	Good result despite weak decision
Resource error	Data, calibration, implementation, routing failure


This prevents CNS from learning the wrong lesson from luck.


